{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"e5fad37d-e707-4d71-a85f-bb41b20a89c5","title":"Free-body diagram of a block on an incline"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"e5fad37d-e707-4d71-a85f-bb41b20a89c5","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Consider a block on a frictionless incline. Its free-body diagram includes only forces exerted on the block.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Block on a frictionless incline"},{"op":"diagram","id":"incline","region":"right","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.1,0.8],[0.9,0.4]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"inclined supporting surface"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.38,0.46],[0.54,0.38],[0.62,0.54],[0.46,0.62],[0.38,0.46]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"block resting on the incline"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.1,0.8],[0.42,0.8]],"stroke":"dashed","meaning":"horizontal reference for incline angle"},{"kind":"arc","center":[0.1,0.8],"radius":[0.18,0.18],"start_deg":-26.565,"end_deg":0,"stroke":"solid","label":"θ"},{"kind":"point","at":[0.5,0.5],"meaning":"block center where force arrows originate"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"e5fad37d-e707-4d71-a85f-bb41b20a89c5","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Gravity points vertically downward. The normal force points perpendicular to the surface, not vertically upward. There is no friction force.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"weight","canvas_id":"incline","from":[0.5,0.5],"to":[0.5,0.85],"stroke":"solid","label":"mg"},{"op":"arrow","id":"normal","canvas_id":"incline","from":[0.5,0.5],"to":[0.36,0.22],"stroke":"solid","label":"N"},{"op":"text","id":"forces","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"Two forces: gravity and normal"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"Does the normal force point vertically upward or perpendicular to the incline?","expected_gist":"Perpendicular to the incline, away from the surface."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"e5fad37d-e707-4d71-a85f-bb41b20a89c5","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Resolve gravity into components parallel and perpendicular to the incline. These dashed arrows replace gravity in calculations; they are not extra forces.","ops":[{"op":"arrow","id":"parallel","canvas_id":"incline","from":[0.5,0.5],"to":[0.36,0.57],"stroke":"dashed","label":"mg sin θ"},{"op":"arrow","id":"inward","canvas_id":"incline","from":[0.5,0.5],"to":[0.64,0.78],"stroke":"dashed","label":"mg cos θ"},{"op":"equation","id":"components","anchor":{"el":"forces","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"W_{\\parallel}=mg\\sin\\theta,\\qquad W_{\\perp}=mg\\cos\\theta"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"e5fad37d-e707-4d71-a85f-bb41b20a89c5","step":{"id":"s4","script":"No acceleration occurs perpendicular to the surface, so the normal balances that component. The unbalanced downhill component accelerates the block.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"balance","anchor":{"el":"components","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"N=mg\\cos\\theta"},{"op":"equation","id":"motion","anchor":{"el":"balance","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"ma=mg\\sin\\theta\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a=g\\sin\\theta"},{"op":"arrow","id":"accel","canvas_id":"incline","from":[0.32,0.4],"to":[0.14,0.49],"stroke":"solid","label":"a downhill"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.done","request_id":"e5fad37d-e707-4d71-a85f-bb41b20a89c5","accepted_steps":4,"repairs":0,"dropped_steps":0,"sanitized_steps":0,"sanitized_fields":0}
